> TLDR: after years of not knowing why my laptops performed worse than I thought they should,
> coding agents helped me finally figure out that Windows apps are broken (like the Razer
> apps or the NVIDIA overlay on your PC right now), but a few scripts and adjustments can fix them
> ad hoc! A feeling I never thought I would have.

Growing up, my friends and I couldn't afford any gaming computers, and having a laptop and a desktop
was out of the question, so we always had to squeeze the last ounce of performance out of whatever
we had. We watched countless videos and read countless guides to get the NVIDIA Control Panel
settings as good as we knew how, and learned what each setting in a game did to find the best
"bang for the buck" setup. As time passed, things like the GeForce app started automating all of
this, and now that I can afford a desktop (well, maybe could, a few years ago, before the RAM
prices), I never bought one: being so far away from everyone, I would only get to use it once a
month. So I still just have a personal laptop, a Legion with a 3070 laptop GPU, which is good enough
for the occasional "hop on Discord to play Peak or Subnautica 2 or anything" with friends.

Today was such a day. We found time, booted up the game, and... 2 FPS?

Normally I would have just gone for a restart, but since I started using this computer as a server,
restarting is a huge hassle, as I have to make sure all the services come back up. Worse, my wife
was already using an app hosted on this computer and I didn't want to interrupt her flow. And with
Opus 5.5 around, I wanted to finally figure out what was going on. I had been burned before by
memory leaks in `audiodg.exe` from Nahimic, so a memory leak and plain thermal throttling were the
first culprits on my mind, but I had never learned how to diagnose these issues properly.

So I opened a terminal, told Opus the game was running at 2 FPS, that I couldn't restart, and to
figure out why. And then I mostly watched (my main contribution to this investigation was approving
permission prompts).

## The usual suspects

It went through pretty much the same list I would have, just a lot faster and with actual numbers
instead of vibes, and it found real problems along the way, just not the one that mattered:

<SuspectList suspects={[
  { name: "Another app on the same GPU", evidence: "real: one of the services I host on this machine was using the GPU way more than it should. Moved it off. 2 → 30 FPS, but still stuttering", guilty: false },
  { name: "Thermals", evidence: "CPU at 94 °C, but still boosting to 3.9 GHz. Not throttling", guilty: false },
  { name: "NVIDIA Overlay leaking memory", evidence: "real: one overlay process from 20 days ago held 12.7 GB, with commit at 48.6 of 49.9 GB. Killed it. Stutter unchanged", guilty: false },
  { name: "Frame pacing, G-Sync", evidence: "turned G-Sync on. Still stuttering, and now I noticed the terminal was too", guilty: false },
  { name: "Something inside the Windows kernel", evidence: "the System process was burning 1.1–1.4 cores nonstop", guilty: true },
]} />

So the memory leak instinct was right: the NVIDIA overlay had been sitting on 12.7 GB for 20 days,
which is exactly the kind of thing a restart would have quietly wiped away without me ever knowing.
Killing it didn't fix anything, though. What actually moved things forward was noticing, while
watching Opus work, that the terminal was stuttering too, which meant the problem wasn't the game at
all. It was something underneath everything.

## Into the kernel

Honestly, this is the point where I would have given up and restarted. I am not even sure I knew you
could get a stack trace of the Windows kernel. Opus ran a 10-second kernel trace with call stacks
(xperf, as admin) and came back with this:

```
dxgmms2.sys!VidMmWorkerThreadProc              75.81%
  dxgmms2.sys!VIDMM_GLOBAL::HandleTrimWnf      75.28%
    ntkrnlmp.exe!NtUpdateWnfStateData          74.68%
      ntkrnlmp.exe!ExpWnfFindScopeInstance     74.30%   (57.9% exclusive)
ntkrnlmp.exe!memcmp                            15.97%   (exclusive)
```

Which, if you are like me, means nothing at first glance, so here is what it is actually saying (or
at least how it was explained to me):

<StallChain title="how a dead program stalls your screen" steps={[
  { plain: "Every window, your game and your terminal included, renders into GPU memory. The Desktop Window Manager (DWM) composites those surfaces into the frame you see.", kernel: "dwm.exe, dxgkrnl.sys" },
  { plain: "GPU memory is shared, so one part of the kernel manages it for everyone: the video memory manager, VidMm.", kernel: "dxgmms2.sys!VidMmWorkerThreadProc" },
  { plain: "When memory gets tight (a game using 7 of 8 GB will do it), VidMm evicts allocations and notifies processes so they can shrink.", kernel: "VIDMM_GLOBAL::HandleTrimWnf" },
  { plain: "That notification goes through WNF, the kernel's internal pub/sub. Before it can publish, the kernel has to find the right scope instance, and it does that by walking a list and comparing entries one by one.", kernel: "ExpWnfFindScopeInstance + memcmp", stat: "74% of System CPU" },
  { plain: "The list was bloated by processes that had exited but never been cleaned up.", kernel: "33,015 process objects, 306 alive", stat: "32,709 zombies" },
  { plain: "While VidMm's worker is busy walking that list, every app waiting on GPU memory waits too, and that includes DWM. So the whole screen hitches, not just the game.", kernel: "System process", stat: "~1.25 cores, nonstop" },
]} />

## 32,709 processes that don't exist

When a program exits but something still holds a handle to it, Windows can't clean it up, and it
sticks around as a zombie. Opus compared how many process objects the kernel had against how many
processes were actually running:

```
Live processes: 306   Process objects in kernel: 33,015   => zombies ~ 32,709
```

The top handle holders were Razer Game Manager (33,695 handles) and Razer Synapse Service (28,193).
Game Manager watches every process you launch so it can tell when you start a game, and it looks like
it opens a handle to each one and never closes it. It had been 20 days since my last restart, so
that is about 1,600 new zombies a day, every one of them making that list a little longer (the
software that makes my mouse glow was slowing down every frame on my screen, great).

Drag the slider back to zero to see what a restart does:

<LinearScan />

A linear search is fine when the list is short, and right after a restart it is. By day 20 every
lookup is about 100 times more expensive, and it happens every time a game pushes on GPU memory.
That's why it creeps up over weeks, that's why a restart "fixes" it, and that's probably why so many
of my gaming sessions over the years started with a restart "just in case".

Opus stopped both Razer services and within a minute the zombies went from 32,709 to 260, the System
process went from about 1.25 cores to 0.2, and the stutter was gone. No restart, and my wife didn't
even notice anything had happened.

To be fair about what's actually proven: the stack and the before-and-after numbers are measured.
That the zombies are what bloats this specific list is an inference. They dropped at the same time
and by about the same factor, but neither Opus nor I have seen the Windows source, so take that part
with a grain of salt.

## Check yours

If you are on Windows, open PowerShell and run:

```powershell
(Get-Counter '\Objects\Processes').CounterSamples[0].CookedValue - (Get-Process).Count
```

A few hundred is normal. If you see thousands, something is leaking, and this will probably tell you
what:

```powershell
Get-Process | Sort HandleCount -Desc | Select -First 5 Name, HandleCount
```

If you run these, please let me know your number and your top offender. I am really curious what
other people find.

## A look behind the curtain

Restarting is still a good idea, because it keeps problems like these from building up in the first
place, but for the first time ever I feel like I got a look behind the curtain. "Game has low FPS"
has always been such an abstract problem, one that made me feel uneasy, but now I can get to an
actual answer. So next time something is going wrong with your computer, I recommend letting an agent loose
to figure it out. What it finds might surprise you, and maybe even teach you a few tricks.
